# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Between Jobs API -- environment variables
#
#   cp .env.example .env     then fill in the two REQUIRED values below
#
# Plain KEY=value lines, one per line: no quotes and no `export` (Docker's --env-file
# reads a line literally, so quotes would become part of the value). A line that starts
# with `# NAME=` is an optional setting shown with its default; delete the `# ` to use it.
# Optional settings that have a default are commented out on purpose: a blank `NAME=`
# is an empty value, not an unset one.
#
# Every variable the API (src/) and the operator scripts (scripts/) read is listed
# here; tests/test_env_example.py fails if the code reads one that is missing. The web
# app and the browser extension have their own files: web/.env.example and
# extension/.env.example.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# --- REQUIRED: Supabase ---
# The API refuses to start without both of these.
#
# Hosted project: dashboard -> Project Settings -> API (the project URL, and the
# service_role secret).
# Local stack (`supabase start`): run `supabase status -o env`; API_URL and
# SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are the two values (the URL is http://127.0.0.1:54321).
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# The service-role key bypasses row-level security: this backend is a trusted server
# (channels and the web app talk to this API, never to Postgres directly), so it has to.
# Treat it like a password. NEVER the anon/publishable key, never in the web app or the
# extension, never in git.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- OPTIONAL: the resume engine (forge-engines) ---
# Base URL of the HTTP service that generates resumes and cover letters. Default
# http://localhost:5682. The engine is NOT part of this repository yet -- without one
# running, resume and cover-letter generation, the Tailor panel, the gap interview,
# the header preview and interview practice fail with a retryable PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE
# error ("Couldn't reach the resume engine"); see README.md, "What works today". No
# credential goes here: the engine is handed the user's own LLM key per call.
# FORGE_ENGINES_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5682

# --- OPTIONAL: the LaTeX compile service (latex-service/, its own Dockerfile, port 5700) ---
# Where the API sends LaTeX to be compiled to a PDF. Default http://localhost:5700, which
# is only right when both run on the same machine -- set it when the API runs in a
# container or on a platform. /health reports it as `unreachable` until it is right.
# LATEX_SERVICE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5700

# How many PDF compiles this API process sends to the LaTeX service at once (default 2).
# Pdflatex is CPU-bound, so a small number keeps every compile quick; the rest wait their
# turn for up to 30 seconds, then the request fails with a retryable "renderer busy" error.
# A positive whole number; anything else stops the API from starting. Raise it only if the
# LaTeX service has the cores for it.
# LATEX_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- OPTIONAL: request limits ---
# The largest request body the API accepts, in bytes (default 1048576, 1 MiB). A bigger
# body -- declared up front or streamed past the limit -- is refused with a 413
# PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE before it is read into memory. Every route here takes a small JSON
# document, so the default is generous; a positive whole number, anything else stops the
# API from starting.
# MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES=1048576
# The per-user rate limits on the routes that spend money or heavy compute (discovery,
# resume generation, company research, ...) are not environment settings: they are a
# table in src/between_jobs/api/rate_limits.py, enforced in the database. A user over a
# limit gets a 429 RATE_LIMITED with a Retry-After header.

# --- OPTIONAL: Telegram bridge ---
# Set BOTH of the next two values, or leave BOTH empty to run without Telegram (web
# only). With neither set the webhook and the Integrations page's Telegram card are
# off; with only one set the server refuses to start.
# Create a bot with @BotFather (/newbot) and paste its token here.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Shared secret Telegram echoes back on every webhook call so this app can tell a real
# Telegram update from a forged POST to the same URL. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Register the webhook with Telegram once the API has a public HTTPS URL (any tunnel
# works for local development). Export both values in your shell (the script does not
# read this file) and run:
#   python scripts/set_telegram_webhook.py --url https://<your-public-host> --dry-run
#   python scripts/set_telegram_webhook.py --url https://<your-public-host>
# A bot has ONE webhook: use a separate bot for dev and for prod.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The bot's public username (with or without the @), shown on the web app's Integrations
# page so people know which bot to send their link code to. Cosmetic: without it the page
# says "this server's Telegram bot". Ignored when Telegram is off.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=

# --- OPTIONAL: Gmail drafts and reply checking ---
# Not needed to boot the app. Only the Gmail connect and callback routes, the route that
# creates a Gmail draft (push-to-gmail) and the Gmail reply-checker worker read these, at
# request or tick time. Leave them unset and those routes answer SETUP_REQUIRED and the
# reply checker does nothing; everything else is unaffected. All three are needed together.
# Google Cloud Console -> APIs & services -> Credentials -> OAuth 2.0 Client ID (type:
# Web application); enable the plain "Gmail API" for the project. The scopes requested
# are gmail.compose (create a draft) and gmail.readonly (read a thread the app already
# knows, for reply checking). The API never calls Gmail's send endpoint: the human
# sends. Treat the client secret like a password.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Must exactly match one of the "Authorized redirect URIs" registered on that same OAuth
# client. The route is /oauth/gmail/callback on the API's public origin; this is the
# local-development value for the API on port 8012.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8012/oauth/gmail/callback

# --- OPTIONAL: logging ---
# One JSON object per line on stdout. DEBUG, INFO (default), WARNING or ERROR; anything
# else falls back to INFO. It sets this app's own loggers only: third-party libraries
# (httpx, openai, ...) always log at WARNING and above, because their lower levels print
# URLs, headers and prompts. Logs carry ids, counts, error codes and exception types --
# never request bodies, keys, resume/JD text or email -- and exceptions are logged
# without their messages.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- OPTIONAL: background workers ---
# The API runs five workers in its own process (outbox, job registry poller, saved-search
# matcher, Gmail reply checker, hiring-signal cache purge). Each runs unless its DISABLE_*
# variable is set to ANY non-empty value. The check is "is it set", not "is it true":
# DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=false and DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=0 both disable the worker. Leave a
# line out (or empty) to keep that worker running. The test suite sets all five.
# DISABLE_OUTBOX_WORKER=
# DISABLE_JOB_REGISTRY_POLLER=
# DISABLE_SAVED_SEARCH_MATCHER=
# DISABLE_GMAIL_REPLY_CHECKER=
# DISABLE_HIRING_SIGNAL_CACHE_PURGE=
# Not a worker: turns the whole Hiring signals feature off (its routes answer 404
# FEATURE_DISABLED). Same rule -- any non-empty value.
# DISABLE_HIRING_SIGNALS=

# /health reports each worker -- 503 when an enabled one has died, gone stale (no finished
# tick for 3x its interval) or failed every tick for 30 minutes (60 seconds for the outbox)
# -- and probes Supabase, the resume engine and the LaTeX service. The probes are
# informational: they never change the status code. Any non-empty value here skips them
# (they then read "not_checked"), like the worker flags above. The test suite sets it.
# DISABLE_HEALTH_DEPENDENCY_CHECKS=

# --- One worker instance, enforced ---
# The job registry poller, the saved-search matcher and the Gmail reply checker each hold
# a lease in the database, so only one running copy of this API does their work at a time
# -- an old and a new container overlapping in a deploy, or a second replica, would
# otherwise each do all of it (and spend your LLM credit twice). Apply the migrations
# first: without the lease functions those three workers report `lease_unknown` and
# /health answers 503, on purpose.
#   on  (default)  hold a lease
#   off            no lease. ONLY for a single local process or a deliberate
#                  single-container deployment; nothing stops a second copy.
# Anything else stops the API from starting.
# WORKER_LEASES=on

# --- OPTIONAL: scoring prompt override ---
# Path to a plain-text file that replaces the built-in system prompt for batch job-fit
# scoring (Discover). Read on every scoring call. An unset variable, or a file that is
# missing, unreadable or blank, falls back to the built-in prompt. The reply must keep
# the JSON shape the built-in prompt asks for.
# JOB_SCORING_SYSTEM_PROMPT_PATH=

# --- Container only ---
# PORT is read by the Docker image's start command, not by the Python code: the port
# uvicorn listens on inside the container (default 8000). A platform sets its own.
# PORT=8000
# RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_ID is set by the Railway platform and used only as a label in the
# worker-lease holder id; it is never needed anywhere else. Do not set it yourself.
# RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_ID=

# --- Operator scripts only (scripts/); the API never reads these ---
# Do NOT put the first two scripts' variables in .env: those scripts deliberately do not
# load it, so a stale file cannot point them at the wrong project. Export them in the shell
# for one run.
#
# scripts/copy_registry_sample.py copies a sample of the job registry from one Supabase
# project (read only) to another (written). Source and target are explicit:
# SAMPLE_SOURCE_SUPABASE_URL=
# SAMPLE_SOURCE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# SAMPLE_TARGET_SUPABASE_URL=
# SAMPLE_TARGET_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# scripts/delete_account.py takes SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY from the
# shell too (and requires --confirm-ref), for the same reason.
#
# scripts/sign_and_publish_ats_field_map.py signs the browser extension's per-ATS field
# maps. The private signing key (base64 Ed25519) lives OUTSIDE this repository and is
# never given to the running API, which only reads maps that were already signed.
# Unlike the two scripts above, this one does call load_dotenv(), but keep the key out of
# .env anyway. Pass --key-file (a path outside the repository) or export the variable for
# one run. If the variable is set it WINS over --key-file, so unset it when you mean to
# use the file.
# ATS_FIELD_MAP_SIGNING_KEY=
